  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 345 AM MDT TUE OCT 6 2026\nA major pattern shift is expected during the extended period.\nFriday will remain warm and dry, but winds will increase in the\nhigher terrain as an upper level trough approaches from British\nColumbia. A cold front will move across the area on Saturday, \ncooling high temperatures by 15 to 20 degrees from Friday. \nBreezy winds will expand to lower elevations as the front moves \nthrough. \n\nThe trough is favored to close off and dive south through the\nPacific Northwest Saturday into Sunday, then may progress\nfurther south before turning east early next week. With this \nscenario, ensembles have consistently developed significant \nprecipitation to the east of the low center where better \nmoisture will exist. This results in a 30-50% chance of \nprecipitation across central Idaho Saturday night through early\nTuesday with a 10-30% chance further west. However, models have\nvaried considerably with the evolution and track of the low, so\nconfidence in precipitation amounts are low. Temperatures are \nexpected to dip normal Sunday through Tuesday. Cooler air will \nalso mean lower snow levels, allowing for snow on the higher \npeaks. However, similar to the precipitation forecast, how much\ncooler depends on the exact track of the low. Currently, highs \nare favored to be 5 to 10 degrees below normal with snow levels \naround 5500-7000 feet. Winds will remain breezy through Sunday, \nthen start to subside early next week.",
